Original commit body from 2b9ed6d184c6d3f1e4f2749288b4a3b53c43b4be:

auto-pr regenerates .ci/affected-tests.txt during the push so a STALE
manifest -- one missing a test the new diff affects -- cannot reach CI. It did
that by overwriting the committed manifest with the slicer's output, and an
overwrite destroys authored intent.

The slicer has no data-file -> test edge (INV-bigaz): a change to
io_primitives/*.yaml, or any other pure-data input, slices to roughly ONE test
file. An author who deliberately widens the manifest to the tests that
actually exercise the changed data had it narrowed back during the push.

THE OVERWRITE IS INVISIBLE FROM THE AUTHOR'S SIDE, which is why it survived:
the manifest is extended, committed correctly, and replaced afterwards. Found
by reading a CI log that said "1 selected files" for a commit whose own
manifest listed twenty. Re-checked across three merged catalogue PRs -- every
one shipped a 1-file manifest while its notes recorded a working 19-file
extension. So the documented INV-bigaz workaround has never once worked.

THIS IS WI-buhov'S BUG ONE DIRECTORY OVER, and it takes WI-buhov's remedy.
auto-pr used to restore backed-up tracker .ops over the working tree
unconditionally and silently ate concurrent writes; _ops_union_restore_file
fixed it by making the restore a UNION. The manifest path had the identical
shape and no such guard. _manifest_union now sits directly beside it in
scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh -- pure (no git, no network), so it is testable by
sourcing the library exactly as its sibling is. The regen MAY still ADD;
it can never DROP. auto-pr reports how many committed entries it kept that the
slicer did not select, so a future occurrence is visible rather than silent.

THE FIRST CUT OF THIS FIX CONTAINED THE BUG IT WAS FIXING, and that is worth
recording. It selected entries with `grep '^packages/'`. The manifest that
motivated the whole change holds 95 entries, every one a ROOT-LEVEL `tests/...`
path -- so that cut would have written an EMPTY test list and CI would have run
nothing while reporting success: strictly worse than the defect being repaired.
It was caught by checking that the pattern actually matched the real file and
by reading how CI parses the manifest, not by a failing test. A lab note
claiming "CI extracts tests via grep ^packages/" was simply wrong.

The manifest is SECTIONED: CI reads everything after "# === SELECTED_TESTS ==="
as the test list and the region above it as CHANGED_SOURCE_FILES. Entries are
"tests/..." or "packages/<pkg>/tests/..." depending on what was selected, so a
path-prefix match is not a valid reader. Selection is now by SECTION, never by
prefix; everything up to and including the marker is copied VERBATIM from the
regenerated file, preserving both the "# Mode:" line the pre-commit hook gates
on and the CHANGED_SOURCE_FILES section. Both failure modes are pinned as
named tests so the prefix mistake cannot return silently.

A filter that removes EVERYTHING is not believed. The existence check is
cwd-relative, matching this caller's long-standing assumption that auto-pr runs
from the repo root. Were that to break, every path would fail -f, the manifest
would empty, and CI would run nothing while reporting success -- the same
silent loss one level down. An empty filter result falls back to the unfiltered
union instead.

Verified end-to-end against the real repository manifest, not only synthetic
fixtures: 95 committed entries + 1 sliced -> 95 survive, with "# Mode: targeted"
and both section markers intact. 195 auto-pr/smart-test tests pass; smart-test
1,622 passed, ratchet clean.

Governance files (scripts/auto-pr, scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh) -- human
approval obtained for this change and for merging it without --gov.

